---
import ModellingLayout from '@/layouts/ModellingLayout.astro';
---

<ModellingLayout title="Help shape the meaning" description="Bring the language, evidence and exceptions you know from property work. A useful contribution can begin with one definition and one well-explained question.">
  <section aria-labelledby="expertise">
    <h2 id="expertise"><span id="start-small" aria-hidden="true"></span>Professional judgement is modelling input</h2>
    <p>
      You do not need an ontology editor or knowledge of RDF. You can help by
      recognising a misleading term, spotting a missing relationship or explaining
      an exception. Technical specialists can work out the formal representation;
      they cannot replace your knowledge of what happens in practice.
    </p>
    <p>
      In our fictional Harbour Court case, someone notices that a corrected report
      has been mistaken for a new inspection. “The visit was on 12 August; the report
      was corrected on 20 August” is already a precise contribution. It names the
      distinction that needs to survive. It is much more actionable than “the dates
      are confusing”, and it does not prescribe any technical syntax.
    </p>
  </section>

  <figure class="modelling-figure" aria-labelledby="contribution-caption">
    <figcaption id="contribution-caption">From an observation to a reviewable question</figcaption>
    <ol class="modelling-steps">
      <li><h3>Point to the meaning</h3><p>Keep the page link, its candidate version if shown, and the exact definition or relationship you are questioning.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Explain the consequence</h3><p>Describe what somebody might misunderstand or do differently because of the wording.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Supply the challenge</h3><p>Give a fictional counterexample or identify permitted evidence. A clear question is enough; replacement wording is optional.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </figure>

  <section aria-labelledby="choose">
    <h2 id="choose"><span id="review-questions" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="read-diagrams" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="send-feedback" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="bring-evidence" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="after-review" aria-hidden="true"></span>Choose the contribution you can make</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt><a href="/semantic-modelling/contribute/review-a-definition">“This does not quite mean what we say at work.”</a></dt>
      <dd>Practise reviewing a definition, distinguish a wording issue from a missing concept, and prepare feedback that somebody can act on.</dd>
      <dt><a href="/semantic-modelling/contribute/bring-evidence">“We have something that would help explain this.”</a></dt>
      <dd>Prepare a permitted source with its version, purpose and restrictions. Learn why a familiar form is evidence to interpret, not a model to copy unquestioningly.</dd>
      <dt><a href="/semantic-modelling/contribute/what-happens-next">“How will I know what happened to the question?”</a></dt>
      <dd>Understand the distinction between discussion, a proposed change, technical checking and a recorded human decision.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="today">
    <h2 id="today">Where to start today</h2>
    <p>
      Use <a href="/search">site search</a> or browse the
      <a href="/development/property-pack">Property Pack candidate</a> to find a
      familiar topic. Read its definition, examples and status before opening
      technical details. The current candidate is not a finished agreement you are
      expected to endorse, and the Harbour Court teaching case does not describe
      its actual contents.
    </p>
    <p>
      For participating members, the <a href="/development/working-groups/member-guide">member
      guide</a> identifies the working-group arrangements. Where a page exposes
      comments, authorised signed-in participants can leave page-specific feedback.
      Teams is the group discussion route. Neither a comment nor a reaction is a
      vote to adopt the model. Access and availability depend on the relevant page
      and working group; this guide does not create a new submission service.
    </p>
    <p>If you are not a participant, you can <a href="/join">express interest in a working group</a>. You do not need to finish reading this section first.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="boundaries">
    <h2 id="boundaries">Make the example useful without exposing a customer</h2>
    <p>
      Use invented names, addresses and circumstances. Keep personal, customer and
      live transaction information out of public pages and discussions. A fictional
      example should preserve the modelling problem, not quietly carry recognisable
      details from a real case. Bring source files only through the authorised
      intake route, with permission to share them.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>What if two professions disagree?</summary>
      <p>Describe each interpretation and the decision it supports. A disagreement may reveal different legitimate contexts, insufficient evidence or an actual contradiction. Do not force one generic definition just to make the discussion appear settled. The people responsible for the affected meanings need to examine the alternatives.</p>
    </details>
    <p><a href="/semantic-modelling/contribute/review-a-definition">Try a definition review →</a></p>
  </section>
</ModellingLayout>
